[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a concise “plug-in migration checklist” for the two Mapbox-specific UI extensions you mentioned.  
It shows what you can keep **as-is**, what you have to rename, and what you must replace with a MapLibre-compatible version (or an alternative).

---

## 1️⃣ `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw`

| What you have now | What to do for MapLibre | Why it works / what to watch |
|-------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------|
| **Import** `import MapboxDraw from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw'` | **Keep the same import** (the library itself does **not** depend on Mapbox services). | The code only talks to the Map API (`map.addControl`, `draw.add`, …) which is identical in MapLibre. |
| **CSS** `import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw/dist/mapbox-gl-draw.css'` | **Rename the CSS class prefix** in your own overrides (if you have any). The library’s bundled CSS still uses the `mapboxgl-` prefix, but MapLibre’s controls use `maplibregl-`. | The control will still appear, but any custom styling that targets `.mapboxgl-ctrl-draw-*` will no longer match. |
| **Instantiating** `new MapboxDraw({ ... })` | No change. | The constructor options (`displayControlsDefault`, `controls`, `styles`, …) are unchanged. |
| **Adding to the map** `map.addControl(draw, 'top-left')` | No change. | `map.addControl` works the same. |
| **Calling draw methods** (`draw.deleteAll()`, `draw.getAll()`, …) | No change. | API is identical. |
| **Optional: custom draw CSS** | If you have a custom stylesheet that overrides the default draw styles, replace every selector that starts with `.mapboxgl-` → `.maplibregl-`. Example: <br>`.mapboxgl-ctrl-draw-polygon { … }` → `.maplibregl-ctrl-draw-polygon { … }` | Without this rename the overrides will be ignored, and the control will fall back to the default look. |
| **Optional: use a more actively-maintained fork** | You can keep the original package, **or** switch to the community-maintained “Terra Draw” adapter: <br>`npm i terra-draw terra-draw-maplibre-gl-adapter` <br>and then: <br>`import { TerraDrawMapLibreGLAdapter } from 'terra-draw-maplibre-gl-adapter';` <br>`const draw = new TerraDrawMapLibreGLAdapter({ map });` | Terra Draw is a thin wrapper that ships a MapLibre-specific CSS bundle (`terra-draw.css`) and is updated more frequently. Use it if you want a package that explicitly mentions MapLibre. |

### Minimal working example (keeping the original draw package)

```html
<link href="https://unpkg.com/mapbox-gl-draw@1.4.2/dist/mapbox-gl-draw.css" rel="stylesheet" />
<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  import MapboxDraw from 'https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw@1.4.2/dist/mapbox-gl-draw.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  const draw = new MapboxDraw({
    displayControlsDefault: false,
    controls: { polygon: true, trash: true }
  });

  map.addControl(draw);
</script>
```

> **Tip:** If you have a custom stylesheet that overrides draw UI, add a quick “global replace” step in your build (e.g. `sed -i 's/mapboxgl-/maplibregl-/g' src/**/*.css`) so you don’t miss any selector.

---

## 2️⃣ `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`

`mapbox-gl-geocoder` is tightly coupled to the Mapbox Geocoding API (it injects the token, builds the request URL, and expects Mapbox-specific response fields). You must replace it with a **MapLibre-compatible geocoder** and point it at an open geocoding service.

### 2.1 Choose a backend

| Service | Public endpoint (demo) | Production notes |
|---------|------------------------|------------------|
| **Nominatim** (OpenStreetMap) | `https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&...` | Public instance is rate-limited & for low-volume testing only. For production either self-host Nominatim or use a managed provider (MapTiler, Photon, Pelias, etc.). |
| **Photon** (Komoot) | `https://photon.komoot.io/api/?q=...` | Free public endpoint, also rate-limited. |
| **MapTiler Geocoding** | `https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json?key=YOUR_KEY` | Requires a MapTiler API key (free tier available). |
| **OpenRouteService** (also offers reverse geocode) | `https://api.openrouteservice.org/geocode/search?api_key=...` | Requires an API key, generous free tier. |
| **Pelias** (self-host or use a hosted provider) | `https://pelias.example.com/v1/search?...` | Good if you already run a Pelias instance. |

Pick the one that matches your usage, budget, and attribution requirements. The **geocoder UI component** itself can stay the same – you just swap the package and give it a different `fetch` function.

### 2.2 Install the MapLibre-specific wrapper

```bash
npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
# optional: if you need the CSS from the original package
npm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder   # only for the CSS, or copy the CSS manually
```

> **Why a separate package?**  
> `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` is a thin fork that removes the hard-coded Mapbox token handling and adds a `fetch` hook so you can point it at any HTTP geocoder endpoint.

### 2.3 Basic usage (Nominatim example)

```javascript
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css'; // reuse the original CSS

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12
});

/**
 * Helper that converts Nominatim JSON → the format expected by the geocoder UI.
 * The UI only needs `place_name` and `center` (lng, lat).
 */
function nominatimToGeocoderFeature(result) {
  return {
    place_name: result.display_name,
    center: [parseFloat(result.lon), parseFloat(result.lat)],
    // keep the raw result if you need extra properties later
    properties: result
  };
}

/* Create the geocoder control */
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // No `accessToken` property – we’re not using Mapbox.
  placeholder: 'Search places…',
  // The UI will call this function for each query string.
  // Return a Promise that resolves to an array of GeoJSON Feature objects.
  forwardGeocode: async (query) => {
    const url = new URL('https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search');
    url.search = new URLSearchParams({
      q: query,
      format: 'json',
      addressdetails: 1,
      limit: 5
    }).toString();

    const response = await fetch(url);
    const data = await response.json();

    // Convert each Nominatim result to the shape the UI expects.
    return {
      features: data.map(nominatimToGeocoderFeature)
    };
  },
  // Optional: reverse geocode (when the user clicks the “pin” button)
  reverseGeocode: async (point) => {
    const url = new URL('https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/reverse');
    url.search = new URLSearchParams({
      lat: point[1],
      lon: point[0],
      format: 'json',
      addressdetails: 1
    }).toString();

    const response = await fetch(url);
    const data = await response.json();

    return {
      features: [nominatimToGeocoderFeature(data)]
    };
  },
  // Keep the default UI options (you can customize `marker`, `zoom`, etc.)
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

#### What changed compared with the Mapbox version?

| Mapbox version (`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`) | MapLibre version (`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`) | Migration note |
|----------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|----------------|
| `new MapboxGeocoder({ accessToken, ... })`   | `new MapLibreGeocoder({ ... })` (no token)       | Remove the `accessToken` field. |
| `geocoder.on('result', e => …)`              | Same event name (`result`)                        | No change. |
| `geocoder.setInput('…')`                     | Same method                                       | No change. |
| `geocoder.clear()`                           | Same method                                       | No change. |
| `geocoder.addTo(map)`                        | Same method (`map.addControl(geocoder)`)          | Prefer the `addControl` pattern, but `addTo` still works because the class extends `IControl`. |
| Internally it builds URLs like `https://api.mapbox.com/geocoding/v5/mapbox.places/...` | You provide the whole request via `forwardGeocode` / `reverseGeocode` callbacks | You now have full control over the endpoint and request parameters. |

### 2.4 If you prefer a drop-in replacement (no custom callbacks)

`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` ships with a **built-in “Mapbox-compatible” mode** that talks to any service that follows the Mapbox Geocoding API contract (e.g., MapTiler Geocoding). If you sign up for a MapTiler key you can do:

```javascript
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // MapTiler’s endpoint mimics Mapbox’s response format
  api: 'https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding',
  key: 'YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'   // <-- no Mapbox token needed
});
```

That way you keep the same UI and the same request shape, just a different host.

### 2.5 CSS considerations

* The original CSS file (`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css`) still works; it only references `.mapboxgl-` class prefixes.  
* If you want the CSS to match MapLibre’s control prefix, run a one-time replace in your build step:  

```bash
# Example with a simple sed (Unix/macOS)
sed -i '' 's/mapboxgl-/maplibregl-/g' node_modules/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css
```

* Or copy the CSS into your own file and edit the prefixes manually. The visual impact is tiny (just the container’s class name), but it avoids “un-styled control” warnings.

---

## 3️⃣ Quick “migration checklist” for the two plugins

| Step | Action | Command (if needed) | Verify |
|------|--------|---------------------|--------|
| **A** | Remove any `mapboxgl.accessToken = '…'` line

### What’s happening?

1. **`map.loadImage` no longer accepts a callback** –  
   Starting with **MapLibre GL v4.0.0** the method was changed to **return a `Promise`** instead of taking a `(err, image)` callback. Because the callback is ignored, the image never gets added, so any symbols that rely on `map.addImage('pin', …)` disappear.

2. **Your marker style is probably pointing at that image** –  
   If the image never gets added, the `icon-image: 'pin'` (or whatever name you use) in your layer/style can’t be rendered, so the markers appear as “missing icons”.

---

## Fix it – use the promise API (or async/await)

```js
// ❌ Old Mapbox-style code – does nothing in MapLibre v4+
map.loadImage(url, (err, img) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  map.addImage('pin', img);
});
```

### Option 1 – `.then()` / `.catch()`

```js
map.loadImage(url)
  .then(({ data }) => {
    // `data` is an HTMLImageElement (or ImageBitmap in newer browsers)
    map.addImage('pin', data);
  })
  .catch(err => {
    console.error('Failed to load marker image:', err);
    // handle the error – maybe fall back to a default icon
  });
```

### Option 2 – `async/await`

```js
async function addPinImage(url) {
  try {
    const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(url);
    map.addImage('pin', img);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Failed to load marker image:', err);
  }
}

// Call it after the map is ready (e.g. in the `load` event)
map.on('load', () => {
  addPinImage('https://example.com/pin.png');
});
```

> **Tip:** `map.loadImage` resolves with an object `{ data, cacheKey }`. The `data` property is the image you pass to `addImage`.

---

## Where to call it

- **After the map has emitted `load`** (or any later point when the style is ready).  
  If you try to add the image *before* the style is loaded, MapLibre will reject the call with `Error: Style is not loaded`.

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // you can add multiple images here
  addPinImage('https://my.cdn.com/icons/pin.png');
});
```

---

## Verify the style uses the image name

Make sure the layer that draws the markers references the exact name you added:

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-pins',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'my-points',
  layout: {
    'icon-image': 'pin',          // <-- must match the name used in addImage()
    'icon-size': 0.8
  }
});
```

If you renamed the image (e.g., `map.addImage('my-pin', img)`), update the `icon-image` accordingly.

---

## Why the icons disappeared after the migration

| Symptom | Typical cause after swapping to MapLibre |
|---------|------------------------------------------|
| “Missing marker icons” / blank symbols | `map.loadImage` callback never ran → image never added |
| Console error: *callback passed to map.loadImage is not being called* | Using the old callback signature on a version that only returns a promise |
| 401/403 errors on the image URL | CORS or authentication issues – make sure the image URL is publicly reachable (no Mapbox token needed) |

---

## Quick checklist for a clean migration of marker icons

1. **Remove every `mapboxgl.accessToken` line** – MapLibre does not need a token.
2. **Replace all `mapboxgl` references with `maplibregl`** (including CSS class prefixes if you style controls manually).
3. **Update `map.loadImage` calls** to the promise form (as shown above).
4. **Confirm the style URL is not a `mapbox://` URL** – use an open style (e.g., OpenFreeMap, MapTiler, your own style JSON) that points to vector/raster sources you control.
5. **Check CSS class names** – if you have custom CSS that targets `.mapboxgl-…`, rename to `.maplibregl-…`.
6. **Test the map after the `load` event** – add images, layers, and controls only after the style is ready.

---

## Full minimal example (ES-module build)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – marker image migration</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position: absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';

  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Wait for the style to be ready
  map.on('load', async () => {
    // 1️⃣ Load the image (promise API)
    try {
      const { data: img } = await map.loadImage(
        'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-js/main/docs/assets/pin.png'
      );
      map.addImage('pin', img);
    } catch (e) {
      console.error('Image load failed', e);
      return;
    }

    // 2️⃣ Add a source of points
    map.addSource('my-points', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: {
        type: 'FeatureCollection',
        features: [
          {
            type: 'Feature',
            geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-122.42, 37.78] },
            properties: {}
          }
        ]
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ Add a symbol layer that uses the image
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-pins',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'my-points',
      layout: {
        'icon-image': 'pin',
        'icon-size': 0.8,
        'icon-allow-overlap': true
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run the snippet (or copy it into a local file) and you’ll see the pin icon appear – confirming that the promise-based `loadImage` works and that the marker layer is correctly wired.

---

### TL;DR

* **`map.loadImage` → returns a Promise**. Convert your callback code to `.then()` or `await`.  
* Call it **after the `load` event** and add the image with the same name you reference in `icon-image`.  
* Once the image is added, the markers will render again.

If you still see blank symbols after making these changes, double-check:

* The image URL is reachable (no 401/403, CORS allowed).  
* The `icon-image` name matches exactly the name used in `addImage`.  
* No leftover `mapboxgl-` CSS classes are interfering with the control styling.

That’s all you need to get your custom marker icons back after moving from Mapbox GL JS to MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping! 🚀

Below are the two concrete things that will break when you lift a **Mapbox GL JS v2** map into the **latest maplibre-gl** (v6 at the time of writing) and the exact changes you need to make.

---

## 1️⃣ `preserveDrawingBuffer` and `antialias` → `canvasContextAttributes`

In Mapbox GL JS these options were top-level `Map` constructor properties:

```js
new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/...',   // (you’ll replace this later)
  preserveDrawingBuffer: true,
  antialias: true,
  // …
});
```

**MapLibre v5+ moved them under a nested `canvasContextAttributes` object** (the same object that also holds `failIfMajorPerformanceCaveat`, `alpha`, etc.). If you keep them at the top level the map will still create, but the flags are ignored and you’ll see a warning in the console.

### What to do

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  // 👉 replace the style URL with a non-Mapbox one (see step 3 of the migration guide)
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty',
  canvasContextAttributes: {
    preserveDrawingBuffer: true,   // now inside the nested object
    antialias: true                // same
    // you can also add: failIfMajorPerformanceCaveat: false, alpha: true, etc.
  },
  // any other Map options you already use (center, zoom, etc.)
});
```

> **Why?**  
> * `canvasContextAttributes` is passed straight to the underlying WebGL context (`getContext('webgl2', …)`).  
> * Keeping the old top-level keys silently drops them, which can break screenshots, export-to-image, or any custom shader that expects antialiasing.

---

## 2️⃣ Chaining `map.on()` no longer works

Mapbox GL JS returned the `Map` instance from `map.on()`, so you could write:

```js
map.on('load', onLoad)
   .on('click', onClick);
```

**MapLibre v5.0.0 changed the return value** of `on()` to a *subscription* object (the same object you get from `map.off()`), so the chain breaks and you’ll get a runtime error like:

```
TypeError: map.on(...).on is not a function
```

### What to do

Call `on()` separately for each event (or store the subscription if you need to later `unsubscribe`):

```js
// Separate calls – the simplest and most common pattern
map.on('load', onLoad);
map.on('click', onClick);

// If you like the “subscription” style, you can keep the return value:
const loadSub   = map.on('load', onLoad);   // returns a subscription
const clickSub  = map.on('click', onClick); // another subscription

// Later, to remove them:
loadSub.unsubscribe();
clickSub.unsubscribe();
```

> **Tip:** If you have many listeners you can loop over an array:

```js
[['load', onLoad], ['click', onClick], ['move', onMove]].forEach(
  ([event, handler]) => map.on(event, handler)
);
```

---

## Quick “Before → After” Summary

| Mapbox GL JS v2 code | MapLibre v6 equivalent |
|----------------------|------------------------|
| ```js<br>new mapboxgl.Map({<br>  preserveDrawingBuffer: true,<br>  antialias: true,<br>  …<br>});``` | ```js<br>new maplibregl.Map({<br>  canvasContextAttributes: {<br>    preserveDrawingBuffer: true,<br>    antialias: true<br>  },<br>  …<br>});``` |
| ```js<br>map.on('load', a).on('click', b);``` | ```js<br>map.on('load', a);<br>map.on('click', b);``` |

---

## 🎯 What to check next (the rest of the migration)

1. **Remove the access token** (`mapboxgl.accessToken = …`) – MapLibre does not need it.  
2. **Replace any `mapbox://` style or source URLs** with a non-Mapbox style (e.g. OpenFreeMap, MapTiler, your own TileJSON).  
3. **Swap the namespace** – `mapboxgl` → `maplibregl` everywhere (including CSS class prefixes).  
4. **Watch for v2-only APIs** (`setFog`, `getFreeCameraOptions`, etc.) – use `setSky` or the camera-calculation helpers described in the migration guide.  
5. **Update plugins** – most still work, but `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` needs its CSS class constants overridden, and `mapbox-gl-compare` should be swapped for `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-compare`.  
6. **Validate your style JSON** – run `npx gl-style-validate style.json` to catch stray `mapbox://` references, missing sprites, or glyph URLs.

---

## TL;DR Code Snippet

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // non-Mapbox style
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],
  zoom: 12,
  canvasContextAttributes: {
    preserveDrawingBuffer: true,
    antialias: true
  }
});

function onLoad() { console.log('map loaded'); }
function onClick(e) { console.log('clicked', e.lngLat); }

map.on('load', onLoad);
map.on('click', onClick);
```

That’s all you need to fix the two issues you mentioned. After these changes, run the app, open the browser console, and verify that no “mapbox://” or “401” errors appear. If you hit any other Mapbox-specific calls (e.g., `setFog`, `mapboxgl.accessToken`, or a Mapbox-only plugin), follow the rest of the migration guide or ask me for the specific replacement. Happy mapping!

Below is a quick-reference guide that shows **all the practical ways you can add a search-box (geocoder) to a map that is built with Mapbox GL JS v2** – and, where relevant, how you can keep the same UI after you move to MapLibre GL JS.

| Option | Who provides it? | What you need to call / pay for | Does it work out-of-the-box with Mapbox GL JS v2? | What you have to change for MapLibre GL JS | Typical usage limits / licensing |
|--------|------------------|--------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|
| **Mapbox Geocoder Control** (`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`) | Mapbox (commercial) | Mapbox Geocoding API – requires a **Mapbox access token** (free tier 100 k requests / month, paid plans for higher volume) | ✅ Yes – this is the “official” control that ships with Mapbox GL JS examples. | Replace the plugin with the **MapLibre-compatible fork** `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` (same API) **or** keep the same code if you keep using Mapbox services. | Free tier, then pay-as-you-go. Must show Mapbox attribution. |
| **MapLibre Geocoder Control** (`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`) | Community fork of Mapbox’s control | Any geocoding service that returns the **Mapbox-style JSON** (`features` array with `place_name`, `center`, `bbox`, etc.) – e.g. Nominatim, Photon, Pelias, MapTiler Geocoding, OpenCage, etc. | ❌ Not a Mapbox-GL-JS-v2 built-in, but works **exactly the same** once you install the package. | Same code as the Mapbox control; just change the import and point the `api`/`url` option at your chosen service. | Depends on the service you pick (most are free for low volume, some have commercial plans). |
| **Self-hosted Nominatim** (OpenStreetMap) | OSM community | Run the Nominatim server yourself (or use the public demo for testing only). | ✅ Works with the MapLibre control (or a custom UI). | Same as above – just point the geocoder’s `url` to your Nominatim endpoint. | No per-request fee, but you must obey the OSM attribution policy and respect rate limits on the public demo. |
| **Photon** (Komoot) | Open-source, hosted by Komoot (free demo) | HTTP GET `/api?` endpoint that returns GeoJSON-style features. | ✅ Works with the MapLibre control (or a tiny wrapper). | Same as above – set `url: "https://photon.komoot.io/api/"`. | Free for low-volume; for production you should self-host. |
| **Pelias** (Mapzen/Geocode-Earth) | Open-source, commercial hosted by Geocode Earth | HTTP GET `/v1/search?` endpoint. | ✅ Works with the MapLibre control (or a tiny wrapper). | Same as above – set `url` to your Pelias endpoint. | Free tier (10 k req/mo) then paid. |
| **MapTiler Geocoding** | MapTiler (commercial) | API key (free tier 100 k req/mo). | ✅ Works with the MapLibre control (or the Mapbox control if you keep the token). | Same as above – set `apiKey`/`url`. | Free tier, then paid. |
| **OpenCage Geocoder** | OpenCage (commercial) | API key (free tier 2 500 req/day). | ✅ Works with the MapLibre control (or a custom UI). | Same as above – set `url` and adapt the response format (OpenCage returns slightly different field names; you can use the `transformResponse` option). | Free tier, then paid. |
| **Algolia Places** (deprecated) | Algolia (now discontinued) | No longer maintained – not recommended for new projects. | ❌ Not recommended. | — | — |
| **Custom UI + Direct API Calls** | Anything (you write it) | Any HTTP client (fetch/axios) + any geocoder service. | ✅ You can build a tiny `<input>` + dropdown yourself. | Same as above – just replace the Mapbox-specific control with your own component. | Depends on the service you call. |

---

## 1️⃣ Quick “plug-and-play” with the **official Mapbox control** (still works in v2)

```bash
npm install @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder
# or with Yarn:
# yarn add @mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder
```

```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';
import MapboxGeocoder from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder';
import '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder/dist/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css';

// Your Mapbox token (required)
mapboxgl.accessToken = 'pk.eyJ1Ijoi...your-token...';

const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],
  zoom: 12
});

const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
  accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken,
  mapboxgl: mapboxgl,          // tells the control which map library to use
  placeholder: 'Search places',
  // optional: limit results to a country, bbox, etc.
  // countries: 'us',
  // bbox: [-123, 36, -121, 38],
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

*Pros*  

* Zero-code UI – the control ships with a ready-made dropdown, keyboard navigation, and mobile-friendly layout.  
* Works with Mapbox’s **autocomplete** (type-ahead) and **reverse-geocode** features out of the box.  

*Cons*  

* Ties you to the **Mapbox Geocoding API** (you need a token, you’re subject to Mapbox’s rate limits and attribution).  
* If you ever migrate to MapLibre, you’ll have to swap the package (see next section).

---

## 2️⃣ Switch to the **MapLibre-compatible fork** (recommended if you plan to leave Mapbox)

The MapLibre community maintains a **drop-in replacement** that uses the exact same constructor signature, but lets you point the control at any geocoder that follows the Mapbox-style response format.

```bash
npm install @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder
```

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';
import '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css';

// Example: using the free public Nominatim instance (good for demos only)
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // No accessToken needed for Nominatim
  // If you use a service that needs a key, pass it here:
  // apiKey: 'YOUR_KEY',
  // The URL of the geocoder endpoint (must return Mapbox-style JSON)
  url: 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&addressdetails=1',
  // Optional: transform Nominatim’s GeoJSON into Mapbox-style features
  // (MapLibreGeocoder will call this for each raw result)
  // If you use a service that already returns Mapbox-style, omit this.
  transformResponse: (response) => {
    // Nominatim returns a FeatureCollection of GeoJSON features.
    // We need to rename `lon`/`lat` → `center` and add `place_name`.
    return response.features.map(f => ({
      ...f,
      center: [f.geometry.coordinates[0], f.geometry.coordinates[1]],
      place_name: f.properties.display_name,
    }));
  },
  placeholder: 'Search OSM places',
  maplibregl: maplibregl,   // tells the control which map library to use
});

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://tiles.openfreemap.org/styles/liberty', // any non-Mapbox style
  center: [-122.44, 37.76],
  zoom: 12
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

### Why this works

* The **control UI** (`<input>`, dropdown, keyboard handling) is exactly the same as the Mapbox version, so you don’t have to redesign your UI.  
* The **`url`** option can point at any HTTP endpoint that returns a JSON payload shaped like Mapbox’s Geocoding API (i.e. an object with a `features` array, each feature having `center`, `place_name`, optional `bbox`, etc.).  
* If the service you pick uses a different schema (e.g. Nominatim’s GeoJSON), you can supply a `transformResponse` function to reshape the data.

---

## 3️⃣ Using **Nominatim** (the most common free open-source geocoder)

### a) Quick-and-dirty public demo (good for prototypes)

```js
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  url: 'https://nominatim.openstreetmap.org/search?format=geojson&addressdetails=1',
  placeholder: 'Search OSM',
  // Nominatim returns GeoJSON, so we need a tiny transformer:
  transformResponse: (response) => {
    return response.features.map(f => ({
      ...f,
      center: f.geometry.coordinates,
      place_name: f.properties.display_name,
    }));
  },
  maplibregl: maplibregl,
});
```

> **⚠️** The public `nominatim.openstreetmap.org` instance is **rate-limited** and **must not be used in production**. See the [Nominatim usage policy](https://operations.osmfoundation.org/policies/nominatim/).

### b) Self-hosted Nominatim (production-ready)

1. **Install** Nominatim on a Linux server (Docker image is the easiest).  
2. **Expose** the HTTP API (default port 8080).  
3. Point the geocoder control at your own URL:

```js
const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  url: 'https://my-nominatim.example.com/search?format=geojson&addressdetails=1',
  transformResponse: (resp) => resp.features.map(f => ({
    ...f,
    center: f.geometry.coordinates,
    place_name: f.properties.display_name,
  })),
  maplibregl,
});
```

*Pros*  

* No third-party fees, full control over data freshness.  
* You can enable extra features (e.g. custom POI layers, language bias).  

*Cons*  

* Requires a server with **≥ 8 GB RAM** and a PostgreSQL/PostGIS database.  
* You must keep the data up-to-date (periodic OSM extracts).  

---

## 4️⃣ Using **Photon** (Komoot’s open-source geocoder)

Photon returns results already in a **Mapbox-compatible shape** (no transformer needed).

```js
